{"number": 173, "text": "               alot        much          many\n\n\n                       =                           Sem,                  Hi |\n                  eke                                                                |\n                       0\n\n\n                                  4      Sig                      i|       I\n                                                 ls                                             ow\n\n\n                    a lot of money       not much  money         a lot of books        not many  books\n\n\n                 We use much  + noncount noun                We  use many + plural noun\n                 (much food / much money,  etc.):            (many  books / many people, etc.):\n                     ©.  Did you buy much food?                   ©. Did you buy many books?\n                     CQ) We don't have much luggage.              ©. We  don’t know many people.\n                     ©)  How  much  money do you want?               How  many  photos did you take?\n                     ©   A: Do you have any money?                ©     Did you take any photos?\n                         B: | have some, but not much.                B: | took some, but not many.\n\n                 We  use a lot of + both types of noun:\n                     ©.  We  boughta lot of food.                     We bought a lot of books.\n                     ©)  Paula doesn’t have a lot of free time.   ©.  Did they ask a lot of questions?\n                 We  say:\n                      ©  There is a lot of food/money/water ...   ©   There are a lot of trees/stores/\n                         (singular verb)                              people ... (plural verb)\n                                                                  ©   Alot of people speak English.\n                                                                      (not speaks)\n\n                 Much and many  are more usual in questions and negative sentences:\n                     ©.  Do you drink much coffee?\n                               Avinl much eaffoo\n                     ©.  Do you have many friends?\n                     ©.  We don't have many friends.\n                 We use a lot of in all types of sentences (affirmative, negative, questions):\n                     ©.  Idrink a lot of coffee.\n                         We don’t have a lot of friends.\n                         Doyou  know a lot of people?\n                 Much  is especially unusual in affirmative sentences:\n                         leat alot of fruit. (not much fruit)\n                     Q   “Do you eat much fruit?” “Yes, a lot.’\n          C      You can use much and a lot without a noun:\n                         Rachel spoke to me, but she didn’t say much.\n                      ©  “Do you watch TV much?”   “No, not        (= not often)\n                         We  like movies, so we go to the movies a lot. (not go to the movies much)\n                               like him very much.\n                   count/noncount => Units 65-66", "char_count": 3009, "mean_conf": 78.9, "word_count": 390, "words": [{"text": "a", "x0": 449, "y0": 848, "x1": 471, "y1": 873, "conf": 94, "bold": false}, {"text": "lot", "x0": 485, "y0": 835, "x1": 541, "y1": 872, "conf": 94, "bold": true}, {"text": "of", "x0": 553, "y0": 833, "x1": 597, "y1": 871, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "money", "x0": 606, "y0": 843, "x1": 748, "y1": 877, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "not", "x0": 911, "y0": 834, "x1": 983, "y1": 864, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "much", "x0": 996, "y0": 825, "x1": 1113, "y1": 863, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "money", "x0": 1128, "y0": 832, "x1": 1270, "y1": 868, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "a", "x0": 1440, "y0": 829, "x1": 1462, "y1": 855, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "lot", "x0": 1476, "y0": 817, "x1": 1533, "y1": 854, "conf": 95, "bold": true}, {"text": "of", "x0": 1545, "y0": 815, "x1": 1589, "